Git Fetch vs Git Pull (Interview + Practical Notes)
Git Fetch	Git Pull
Downloads the latest changes from the remote repository.	Downloads the latest changes and merges them into your current branch.
Does not modify your working files.	Updates your working files after merging.
Safe to use before reviewing changes.	Directly updates your local branch.
Used when you want to inspect changes before merging.	Used when you want to immediately synchronize your branch.
1. git fetch
What it does

Downloads the latest commits, branches, and tags from the remote repository but does not merge them into your current branch.

Command
git fetch origin
Industry Use Case

You start your work in the morning and want to see what your teammates have pushed.

git fetch origin

Now you can review the changes before deciding whether to merge them.

Best when:

Reviewing team changes
Checking updates safely
Avoiding unexpected merge conflicts
2. git pull
What it does

Downloads the latest changes from the remote repository and immediately merges them into your current branch.

Command
git pull origin main

Internally, Git performs:

git fetch
git merge
Industry Use Case

Before starting development, you want your local main branch to match the latest code on the remote repository.

git checkout main
git pull origin main

Now your local branch is up to date and ready for development.

Best when:

You trust the remote changes
You want the latest code immediately
Starting work or before creating a new feature branch
Visual Example
Before
Remote (origin/main)

A ---- B ---- C ---- D

Local (main)

A ---- B ---- C
After git fetch
Remote (origin/main)

A ---- B ---- C ---- D

Local (main)

A ---- B ---- C

(Your local branch is unchanged, but Git knows about commit D.)
After git pull
Remote (origin/main)

A ---- B ---- C ---- D

Local (main)

A ---- B ---- C ---- D

Your local branch and working files are updated.

Interview Answer (30 Seconds)

Q: What is the difference between git fetch and git pull?

Answer:

git fetch downloads the latest changes from the remote repository without changing my local branch or working files. It allows me to review updates before merging. git pull performs a fetch followed by a merge, updating my current branch and working directory with the latest remote changes.

